                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Read the portfolio: program-level contribution`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `You hold a program-by-program P&L for all <strong>14 open-enrollment offerings</strong> plus the custom book. No finance lecture is given — you work it out. Compute <em>contribution margin per program</em>, see which programs subsidize which, and separate the genuinely loss-making from the merely low-margin with pricing power. The trap: the <strong>PAD flagship</strong> bills S/ 720K against S/ 720K of cost — <em>0% contribution</em> on your two scarcest faculty — yet it is the alumni network and the engine of your highest-margin custom book. Classify the portfolio and deliver a one-line verdict on the flagship.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Reprice the open-enrollment portfolio`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Lift blended contribution toward the <strong>35% floor</strong> through pricing — not slogans. Elasticity is roughly <em>−0.7</em>: a 10% price rise historically cuts enrollment 6–8%, and it varies by program. A uniform hike treats 14 distinct programs as one product and drops your fill rate; deep discounting buys volume at the cost of margin and brand. Premium-up the programs with genuine pricing power, hold or modestly cut the elastic mid-tier, and protect the corporate-sponsored learners who feed repeat custom. And remember: in executive education the experience <em>is</em> the product — slashing variable spend to make the number is a slow brand decline, not margin.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Custom vs open mix & the client renegotiation`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Deploy scarce faculty between higher-margin-but-lumpy <strong>custom work</strong> and steadier open-enrollment. Your two top faculty are at <em>95% utilization nine months out</em> — the binding constraint, not market demand, governs how fast you can grow. A key mining client (18% of custom, ~S/ 1.94M) demands a <strong>12% rate cut</strong>, putting S/ 470K of your best-margin contribution at risk. Conceding the logo for nothing surrenders the contribution and the scarce faculty days; counter for a multi-year term and a volume commitment that more than offsets the cut. Fund a vetted adjunct bench so growth does not erode quality.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Approve the capacity investment`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The adjacent floor — <strong>+2 classrooms, +33% capacity</strong> — can be leased at <em>S/ 1.4M/year</em> (opex) or owned for <em>S/ 5.2M</em> (capex); the lease must be signed within 120 days. Build the investment case: incremental contribution from filling the new space, <strong>ROI</strong> and <strong>payback</strong>, at three demand scenarios. Your existing rooms run at 62% — committing S/ 5.2M of capex on an optimistic 75% ramp stretches payback past six years and breaches the floor while you pay it down. Compute the <em>break-even fill rate</em> first, compare it to today's 62%, and gate the decision on it.`,

                    r1_classify_label:      `How do you classify the open-enrollment portfolio against the 35% floor?`,
