                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_segment:       `<strong>That is the contribution lens.</strong> Ranking each program by contribution margin against its fill rate is how you see which offerings fund the mission and which destroy value. Now you can act on the few programs that matter instead of the blended average that hides them.`,
                    coach_r1_single:        `<strong>“The blended 31% is the only number” is exactly the trap.</strong> Your portfolio is 14 programs with wildly different margins and fill. Act on the average and you will raise prices on the wrong ones and protect the wrong ones. The average hides the decisions.`,
                    coach_r1_cutall:        `<strong>“Anything below 35% is loss-making” confuses low-margin with value-destroying.</strong> A program at 28% contribution still throws off cash and fills seats; cutting it loses contribution you needed. Separate the genuinely loss-making from the merely low-margin with pricing power.`,
                    coach_r1_padstrategic:  `<strong>Read correctly.</strong> The PAD is not a profit center — it is your demand-generation engine. Its alumni feed your highest-margin custom book. Retain it as a <em>consciously cross-subsidized</em> asset funded by higher-margin programs, an explicit portfolio choice rather than an accident.`,
                    coach_r1_padkill:       `<strong>You killed the PAD to save S/ 720K of cost.</strong> Next year, custom leads from PAD alumni fall ~30% — roughly S/ 1.3M of your best-margin custom book. The flagship was not a profit center; it was your demand engine. What did you actually save?`,
                    coach_r1_padignore:     `<strong>Leaving the flagship unexamined is not the same as a verdict.</strong> Retaining the PAD can be right — but only as a deliberate, costed cross-subsidy you can defend to the rector. “Do not question it” is how a 0% program quietly consumes your scarcest faculty unchallenged.`,
                    coach_r1_overhead:      `<strong>Judging programs on profit after full allocated overhead overstates the case to cut.</strong> Allocated overhead does not disappear when a program closes — it lands on the survivors. The decision lens is <em>contribution</em>, before allocated overhead.`,
                    coach_r1_revenue:       `<strong>Ranking by revenue alone tells you nothing about value.</strong> Your highest-revenue program can be your worst contributor. Revenue is vanity; contribution is the number that funds the mission.`,
                    coach_r1_norigor:       `<strong>Acting on instinct over a 14-program book is how the average misleads you.</strong> Without a real contribution map you cannot see which programs have pricing power and which are loss-making. Build the map before you touch a price.`,
                    coach_r2_segmented:     `<strong>Segmented repricing is the disciplined move.</strong> You priced to elasticity, not to a slogan — a modest premium where there is pricing power, restraint on the elastic mid-tier, and protection for the sponsored learners who feed your custom book. Margin rises and fill holds.`,
                    coach_r2_uniform:       `<strong>You lifted every list price 12% to close the gap.</strong> Enrollment in three mid-tier certificates fell ~9% and your fill dropped from 62% toward 55% — so contribution went <em>down</em>, not up. Which programs had pricing power and which did not? You treated the portfolio as one product.`,
                    coach_r2_discount:      `<strong>Discounting hard to fill seats trades your margin and your brand for volume.</strong> In executive education price is a quality signal; cut it across the board and you both lose contribution and tell the market your programs are worth less.`,
                    coach_r2_overpremium:   `<strong>Right instinct, too much premium.</strong> A double-digit rise even on a strong program eventually finds its elasticity — fill slips and the contribution you gained per seat is lost to empty seats. A modest premium is the durable move.`,
                    coach_r2_underpremium:  `<strong>You had pricing power and gave it away.</strong> Cutting price on the programs that could absorb a premium leaves contribution on the table. Reputation programs sit on a price-signal curve where a modest rise can <em>raise</em> perceived quality.`,
                    coach_r2_deepdisc:      `<strong>Deep open discounting makes your list price fiction.</strong> When everyone negotiates, your strongest programs subsidize your discount habit and the brand signal erodes. Discipline the discount before it disciplines your margin.`,
                    coach_r2_nodisc:        `<strong>A rigid no-discount wall prices out the corporate-sponsored learners.</strong> Those sponsored cohorts are the seedbed of your repeat custom business; lose them on principle and you starve the highest-margin line. Targeted discipline beats a blanket rule.`,
                    coach_r2_slash:         `<strong>You reached margin by cutting per-cohort spend ~20%.</strong> NPS just dropped from 72 to 54 and two corporate sponsors flagged “declining quality.” In executive education the experience <em>is</em> the product. Did you raise margin, or start a slow brand decline you will pay for in lost custom?`,
                    coach_r3_balanced:      `<strong>You grew custom to the capacity ceiling, not past it.</strong> Custom carries your best margin, but only as far as your faculty can actually teach it well. Growing into the constraint — not through it — captures the margin without the quality and reputation risk.`,
                    coach_r3_custompush:    `<strong>Booking 40% more custom is margin upside — if someone can teach it.</strong> Your two top faculty are already at 95% utilization nine months out. Who delivers these programs? Demand did not become your constraint; faculty capacity did.`,
                    coach_r3_openonly:      `<strong>Retreating to open-enrollment only surrenders your best margin.</strong> Custom is lumpy, but it is where the contribution is. The discipline is to manage the lumpiness against capacity, not to abandon the line.`,
                    coach_r3_overbook:      `<strong>Overbooking the two stars sells names you cannot deliver.</strong> When the calendar collides, you either cancel on a corporate client or hand the room to someone untested. Either way the reputation cost lands on your highest-margin relationships.`,
                    coach_r3_untested:      `<strong>Staffing a S/ 2M client with untested adjuncts is a reputation bet you did not price.</strong> If the quality slips, you do not just lose this program — you lose the client's trust in the whole custom line. The binding constraint was faculty, and you ignored it.`,
                    coach_r3_capacity:      `<strong>Your plan books 40% more custom against faculty already at 95%.</strong> Who teaches these programs? Bring in untested adjuncts for a marquee client and you are betting brand quality on people you have not vetted. Capacity, not demand, governs this growth.`,
                    coach_r3_counter:       `<strong>That is a counter, not a concession.</strong> You traded a smaller cut for a multi-year term and a volume commitment — the contribution you give on rate, you more than recover on book size and certainty, and you kept your scarce faculty days working at their best margin.`,
                    coach_r3_concede:       `<strong>You gave the mining group its 12% cut — S/ 470K of contribution gone, on your highest-margin line.</strong> Did you get a multi-year commitment or higher volume in return, or did you just protect a logo? And the scarce faculty days you reserved for them — what higher-margin work did you turn away?`,
                    coach_r3_walk:          `<strong>Letting them walk on principle is the opposite error.</strong> You just lost ~S/ 1.94M of your highest-margin revenue rather than negotiate. A flat refusal protects the rate and surrenders the relationship — the counter was the disciplined move, not the binary.`,
                    coach_r3_nobench:       `<strong>Growing custom with zero bench investment bets quality on stretch alone.</strong> The first time the calendar collides you will reach for whoever is free. Fund the vetted bench and QA, or your growth quietly erodes the quality that is the product.`,
                    coach_r4_lease:         `<strong>The lease is the disciplined capacity bet.</strong> S/ 1.4M/year of reversible opex lets you grow into the +33% capacity and exit if demand disappoints — no S/ 5.2M sunk against a fill rate you have not proven. Match the commitment to the certainty of the demand.`,
                    coach_r4_own:           `<strong>Owning can be right — because you scenario-tested it.</strong> You did not build the case on the optimistic ramp; you stress-tested a realistic fill against the floor and the cash. Capex is defensible when the break-even fill sits comfortably below what you can actually deliver.`,
                    coach_r4_ownoptimistic: `<strong>You committed S/ 5.2M of capex assuming the new rooms fill at 75% in year one.</strong> Your <em>existing</em> rooms run at 62%. At a realistic 50% ramp, payback stretches past six years and you breach the margin floor while paying it down. What fill rate does the new space actually need — and are you confident you can hit it?`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>Doing nothing is a defensible answer — if the math says so.</strong> But letting the option expire means living within six classrooms at 62%, and you have given up the cheapest margin you have: filling capacity you already have. Make sure “no” is a decision, not an avoidance.`,
                    coach_r4_optimistic:    `<strong>Building any case on a 75% optimistic ramp is the discipline failure.</strong> Your existing rooms run at 62%; a new wing rarely outperforms the old one in year one. An optimistic scenario flatters every ROI — the one that protects you is the realistic one.`,
                    coach_r4_breakeven:     `<strong>You derived the break-even fill before you committed.</strong> Knowing the new space must hit, say, the low-50s to cover the lease — and that your rooms already run at 62% — turns the decision from a hope into a calculation. That is capital-budgeting discipline.`,
                    coach_r4_noskip:        `<strong>Signing on the headline ROI without the break-even fill is flying blind.</strong> ROI on an optimistic scenario tells you nothing about the downside. The number that protects you is the fill rate the space must reach to break even — compute it before you sign.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Segmented the portfolio; flagship retained as a conscious cross-subsidy; judged on contribution.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Read the portfolio on the average, or killed the flagship on contribution alone.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Segmented repricing, disciplined discounts, quality protected.`,
                    sum_r2_uniform:         `Uniform price hike — fill rate fell, contribution went down.`,
                    sum_r2_quality:         `Slashed variable spend — margin bought with a quality/NPS hit.`,
                    sum_r2_discount:        `Discounted to fill — volume up, margin and brand down.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Pricing or policy under-tuned.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Grew custom to the ceiling, vetted bench, countered the client for value.`,
                    sum_r3_capacity:        `Grew custom past faculty capacity — quality and reputation at risk.`,
                    sum_r3_concede:         `Conceded the 12% cut for nothing — S/ 470K of contribution gone.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Mix or negotiation partially right.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Leased the capacity, scenario-tested, break-even computed first.`,
                    sum_r4_overinvest:      `Owned on the optimistic ramp — payback stretches, floor breached.`,
                    sum_r4_nothing:         `Let the option expire — lived within six classrooms.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Investment case not fully stress-tested.`,

                    insight_funded:         `Program funded: you ran the genuine portfolio-finance pattern — a segmented repricing that lifted blended contribution to the floor, a flagship retained as a conscious cross-subsidy, custom grown to the capacity ceiling, a client renegotiation that traded rate for value, and a capacity bet matched to proven demand. Margin meets the 35% floor without hollowing the brand, fill is healthy, and the contribution funds the mission.`,
                    insight_hollow:         `Hollow margin: you may have reached the number, but you bought it by destroying value — killing the demand-engine flagship or slashing the experience that is the product. The margin on the page is not the margin you will sustain once lost custom leads and declining quality show up in next year's book.`,
                    insight_shortfall:      `Floor missed: blended contribution stayed below 35%. The gap was about S/ 1M on S/ 24M of revenue — closable with segmented pricing and a few points of fill — but the levers you pulled did not add up to it. Miss the floor a second year and the unit is merged and the director role disappears.`,
                    insight_mixed:          `A mixed outcome: some of the finance discipline landed, but pricing, mix and the capacity bet did not fully reinforce each other — enough slack for the rector to question whether the floor is held sustainably or just this once.`,
                    insight_flagship:       `You killed the PAD flagship on its 0% contribution — and lost the alumni-and-custom demand engine behind it. The S/ 720K of cost you saved is dwarfed by roughly S/ 1.3M of higher-margin custom leads that came from PAD alumni. Contribution analysis must be read alongside strategic interdependencies.`,
                    insight_uniform:        `The uniform price hike treated 14 distinct programs as one product. Where you lacked pricing power, enrollment fell and fill dropped — so the across-the-board move lowered contribution instead of raising it. Segmentation and elasticity discipline were the missing lens.`,
                    insight_concede:        `You conceded the mining client's 12% cut without extracting counter-value — S/ 470K of contribution gone on your highest-margin line, with no multi-year term or volume in return, and the scarce faculty days reserved for work you could have priced higher.`,
                    insight_overinvest:     `You committed the S/ 5.2M owned-capacity buildout on an optimistic fill assumption while your existing rooms run at 62%. Payback stretched past the safe horizon and you breached the margin floor while paying it down. Scenario discipline and a break-even fill come before sinking capital.`,
                    insight_capacity:       `You grew custom past your faculty capacity. With your two top instructors already at 95% utilization, the incremental programs landed on overbooked stars or untested adjuncts — putting the quality and reputation of your highest-margin line at risk. The binding constraint, not demand, governs growth.`,
                    insight_quality:        `You reached margin by slashing per-cohort variable spend — and NPS and sponsor confidence fell with it. In executive education the experience is the product; cost-cutting that the learners and sponsors feel is value-destroying, not sustainable margin.`,
                    insight_fillgap:        `You held the margin floor but left capacity on the table: at this fill rate, seats your faculty and rooms could serve sit empty, and an empty seat is lost forever. Raising fill is usually cheaper margin than raising price — the easiest contribution you are not yet capturing.`,

                    hint_flagship:          `You killed the demand-engine flagship — strategic health and your custom pipeline are taking the hit. Contribution must be read with strategic interdependencies; the cheapest margin is often the capacity you already have.`,
                    hint_quality:           `You bought margin by cutting the experience — NPS and sponsor confidence are falling. In executive education the experience is the product; protect it or the margin will not last.`,
